                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose how the informal market actually works`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Your field-research pack lands. Volume runs through a layered web: <strong>big wholesalers → semi-wholesalers → table-top retailers → hawkers</strong>. Price is set by bargaining; posted lists and formal contracts are ignored. A handful of senior traders — the <strong>market leaders</strong> — decide whether a new brand is welcomed or frozen out, and the incumbent locks traders in with relationships and 14-day credit. Before you spend a naira, name the single biggest barrier to entry and find where power really sits. <em>The modern-trade playbook that built your supermarket business is the wrong map here.</em>`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Set the pricing approach`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Design pricing for a channel where <strong>price is negotiated, not posted</strong> (Uzo, 2021). A rigid national list is laughed at and arbitraged within a week. You must stack margin so every tier — wholesaler, semi-wholesaler, retailer — earns enough to <em>push</em> your brand, protect Greenfield's net margin, and decide how to handle the haggle: build deliberate bargaining room into the stack, or hold a firm price. Starve the trade and they stock you but never sell you; give it all away and your own margin collapses. Then set a guardrail against destructive arbitrage.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Choose reseller relationships & the distribution model`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Decide who you partner with and how product physically reaches thousands of tiny, cash-only, low-drop-size outlets. Court the <strong>market leaders</strong> first (fast credibility, but dependence), build a <strong>broad base</strong> (slower, more control), or <strong>sequence</strong> both. Set a credit policy against an incumbent giving traders 14-day credit. Then allocate the <strong>₦900M</strong> across market sub-distributors, company van-sales routes, wholesale partners and a trade-credit float. <em>Your two formal distributors won't touch outlets buying three units at a time — the informal channel needs a purpose-built route to market.</em>`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Recover & scale: defend the beachhead`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `The incumbent strikes back — a trade-price cut, a stocking bonus to freeze you out, or a key market leader demanding exclusivity. Defend your early penetration <strong>without torching margin or reseller trust</strong>, and reconcile your response with the pricing and credit policies you already set. A street price war or caving to the gatekeeper protects volume today and bleeds the brand tomorrow; <em>in this market, how the deal feels drives loyalty as much as the price</em>. Then show how the Lagos beachhead scales to Onitsha and pitch the integrated go-to-market to the board.`,

                    r1_barrier_label:       `Diagnose the single biggest barrier to entry in the informal channel.`,
                    barrier_trust:          `Trust & credit lock-in — the incumbent's relationships and 14-day credit freeze us out`,
                    barrier_price:          `Price — our landed cost is simply too high to compete on the street`,
                    barrier_distribution:   `Distribution reach — we can't physically get product to thousands of tiny outlets`,
                    barrier_awareness:      `Brand awareness — consumers just don't know the Greenfield name yet`,
                    r1_power_label:         `Where do margin and power concentrate in the trader hierarchy?`,
