                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_trust:         `<strong>You've named the real barrier.</strong> It isn't price or awareness — it's trust and the incumbent's 14-day credit. The trader has a ten-year relationship and credit he won't risk for an unknown brand at cash terms. Relationship capital is the currency of this channel; build your strategy around earning it.`,
                    coach_r1_distribution:  `<strong>Reach matters — but it isn't the deepest barrier.</strong> You can build a route to thousands of outlets, but if the trader doesn't trust you and the incumbent gives him credit, the trucks arrive at a shelf that won't take you. Get the relationship and credit answer right first.`,
                    coach_r1_price:         `<strong>Careful — "we're too expensive" is the modern-trade reflex.</strong> Price matters, but the trader buys on relationship and credit, not on your printed number. Diagnose the trust-and-credit lock-in before you start cutting price you can't afford to cut.`,
                    coach_r1_awareness:     `<strong>Consumers will discover the brand once the trade pushes it.</strong> Awareness is a symptom, not the barrier — the real question is why a wholesaler with a credit line from the incumbent would ever choose to stock and push you.`,
                    coach_r1_distributor:   `<strong>Your two formal distributors do NOT own this route to market.</strong> They serve supermarkets, not table-top sellers buying three units at a time. Believing they hold the power is exactly the modern-trade map that has failed here for three years.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> Trust-and-credit lock-in is the binding barrier, and the senior market leaders are where power concentrates — win their welcome and the chain opens; ignore them and you're frozen out. Now design pricing and relationships around that reality.`,
                    coach_r1_lowunlearn:    `<strong>You're still holding the modern-trade playbook.</strong> Fixed lists, formal contracts, scan-data promotions — none of it travels into Idumota. The less you unlearn, the more your strategy will quietly fail in a channel that runs on negotiation and trust.`,
                    coach_r2_stacked:       `<strong>A margin-stacked structure is built for this channel.</strong> Each tier sees enough margin to push you, and you've left room for the haggle the buyer values. That's pricing designed FOR negotiation, not against it — exactly what Uzo's research points to.`,
                    coach_r2_fixed:         `<strong>You published a firm national price and told the field force to hold it.</strong> The first wholesaler in Idumota laughed — he buys on relationship and volume, not your printout — and the trader next door is already arbitraging it. Your list is ignored within a week. In this market price is negotiated, not posted.`,
                    coach_r2_tiered:        `<strong>Volume tiers are a reasonable start</strong> — they reward bigger buyers and give some retail guidance. But guidance is still a posted logic; to really fit the channel, stack the margin and engineer in the bargaining room the buyer expects.`,
                    coach_r2_relational:    `<strong>Relationship-linked pricing fits the channel's logic</strong> — terms that flex with loyalty and cash are how this market actually trades. Just make sure each tier still earns enough to push you and that flexibility doesn't open the door to arbitrage.`,
                    coach_r2_starve:        `<strong>You priced for a fat Greenfield margin and left the trade thin.</strong> The wholesalers will stock you but never push you — why would they, when the incumbent's brand earns them more per unit? In a push channel, the trade sells what pays the trade. Your margin choice just cost you advocacy.`,
                    coach_r2_givenaway:     `<strong>Generous to the trade — too generous.</strong> You've left so much margin in the chain that Greenfield's own net price is collapsing. Motivating the trade is right; subsidising it into your own losses is not. Find the band where the trade pushes AND you still earn.`,
                    coach_r2_noroom:        `<strong>You stacked the margin but left zero room for the haggle.</strong> In this market the buyer wants to feel they won the negotiation. A price with no give-and-take feels rigid and transactional — engineer in some bargaining room or you'll lose on bargain satisfaction.`,
                    coach_r2_rigid:         `<strong>Policing one rigid street price is the modern-trade reflex again.</strong> You can't punish your way to a fixed price in a channel that bargains — you'll crush the haggle the buyer values and the traders will simply ignore the rule. Guardrails should prevent ruinous arbitrage, not kill negotiation.`,
                    coach_r3_sequenced:     `<strong>Sequencing is the disciplined play.</strong> A few market leaders give you fast credibility and entry; broadening the base afterward dilutes the dependence before any one gatekeeper can squeeze you. You bought speed without permanently surrendering control.`,
                    coach_r3_gatekeepers:   `<strong>You won two powerful market leaders fast — instant credibility and volume.</strong> But now they control your access, and one is already hinting he'll want exclusivity and a bigger cut next quarter. You traded control for speed; what's your leverage when the gatekeeper squeezes you?`,
                    coach_r3_selective:     `<strong>Selective credit is the commercially sane answer to the lock-in.</strong> You match the incumbent where it matters — for loyal, vetted traders — without lending to everyone and blowing your working capital. Just make sure the float actually funds it.`,
                    coach_r3_cash:          `<strong>You insisted on cash-only to protect working capital — disciplined, and you lost the shelf.</strong> The incumbent gives the same traders 14-day credit and a ten-year relationship. The trader has no reason to risk that for your unknown brand at cash terms. Credit is the real currency here.`,
                    coach_r3_aggressive:    `<strong>Out-lending the incumbent to everyone buys the shelf fast — and bleeds working capital.</strong> Credit to traders you haven't vetted is bad debt waiting to happen, and a credit war with an entrenched incumbent is one you finance and they win. Target the credit; don't carpet-bomb it.`,
                    coach_r3_underfunded:   `<strong>Your credit policy has no money behind it.</strong> You promised traders a credit float but barely funded it — the field force will run out of float mid-quarter and the loyalty you were buying evaporates. Relationship and distribution choices are coupled; fund what you promised.`,
                    coach_r4_relationship:  `<strong>That's the defense this market rewards.</strong> You held price and leaned on loyalty, service and the bargaining experience instead of torching margin. The incumbent can cut price, but it can't easily out-relationship a trade that now feels you treat them like family. Trust defended the beachhead.`,
                    coach_r4_matchprice:    `<strong>You went to a street price war and the brand bled.</strong> Matching the incumbent's cut held some volume but collapsed your margin and taught the trade your brand is a discount play. You can't out-cut an entrenched incumbent on price alone — that's the one lever it can always pull harder.`,
                    coach_r4_cave:          `<strong>You out-bid the bonus / caved to keep the shelf — at the cost of your margin.</strong> Buying your way out of every squeeze trains the trade to squeeze you again. Volume held this quarter; the economics didn't.`,
                    coach_r4_squeezed:      `<strong>The gatekeeper you depended on just squeezed you, and you caved.</strong> This is the dependence you bought in Round 3 coming due — exclusivity and a bigger cut, with no leverage because you have no broad base to fall back on. Speed had a price, and you're paying it now.`,
                    coach_r4_retreat:       `<strong>You pulled back to modern trade and protected margin — by ceding the mandate.</strong> The board's whole point was that modern trade has flatlined and the mass market is informal. Retreating to the channel that's failing is exactly how you lose the year's commercial priority.`,
                    coach_r4_rush:          `<strong>Opening every cluster at once is the over-reach trap.</strong> You haven't proven the model holds in one market and you're already national — thin field support, stretched credit and gatekeepers in cities you don't understand. Scale breaks what a beachhead hadn't yet secured.`,
                    coach_r4_holds:         `<strong>That holds together end to end.</strong> A relationship defense plus an adapted scale plan — replicate the RTM and margin logic, re-map each city's gatekeepers — is the path from a Lagos beachhead to real mass-market share, Onitsha included.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Named the trust/credit barrier; located gatekeeper power.`,
