                    insight_secured:        `Beachhead secured: you read the channel as it is — trust and credit as the barrier, gatekeepers as the power, price designed for bargaining, a purpose-built route to market — and defended it on relationship rather than price. That is how a Lagos foothold becomes scalable mass-market share.`,
                    insight_frozen:         `Frozen out: cash-only discipline lost you the shelf to an incumbent extending 14-day credit and a decade of relationship. In a trust-based channel, credit and relationship capital are the binding constraint — the rational finance choice was the commercially wrong one.`,
                    insight_dependent:      `Captured by the gatekeeper: you won the big market leaders fast and then they owned your access. When one demanded exclusivity and a bigger cut you had no broad base to fall back on. Speed bought credibility and surrendered control.`,
                    insight_short:          `You didn't fully adapt to the channel. Pieces of the modern-trade playbook survived into a market that bargains, trusts and lends — enough for the trade to stock you without pushing you, and for the incumbent to hold the shelf.`,
                    insight_margin_low:     `Your net margin fell below the 9% modern-trade baseline — a price war, an over-generous trade margin, or buying your way out of the incumbent's squeeze. Penetration without margin isn't a business; the informal channel was supposed to deliver higher net margin, not less.`,
                    insight_margin_strong:  `Strong net margin: you stacked the trade's incentive without giving away Greenfield's net price, and you defended the beachhead without a margin-torching price war. The channel delivered the higher net margin it was supposed to.`,
                    insight_starved:        `You starved the trade. Pricing for a fat manufacturer margin left wholesalers no reason to push you — in a push channel the trade sells what pays the trade, and yours paid less than the incumbent's.`,
                    insight_relational:     `Loyalty and bargain satisfaction are both high — you competed on how the deal feels, not just the landed price. That relational edge is the moat the incumbent can't easily cut its way through.`,
                    insight_scale:          `Penetration is now at a level that reads as a genuine beachhead. Adapt — don't copy — the model into Onitsha: replicate the RTM and margin logic, but re-map each city's gatekeepers and credit norms.`,

                    hint_cash:              `Cash-only is losing you the shelf to the credit incumbent. Extend selective credit to loyal traders and fund a real trade-credit float, or the relationship — and the volume — goes to the brand that lends.`,
                    hint_starved:           `You've starved the trade. Lift the margin you leave the chain into a band where wholesalers actually push you — in this channel the trade sells what pays the trade.`,
                    hint_dependent:         `You're dependent on a few gatekeepers and your loyalty is thin. Broaden the reseller base now so no single market leader can squeeze you on exclusivity or a bigger cut.`,
                    hint_margin:            `Your net margin is dangerously low. Stop buying volume with price cuts and giveaways — defend on relationship and bargain satisfaction, and protect Greenfield's net price.`,
                    hint_bargain:           `Bargain satisfaction is low — buyers feel your pricing is rigid and transactional. Build deliberate bargaining room into the margin stack so the buyer feels they won the haggle.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — real penetration, healthy margin and loyal resellers. Lock it in by defending on relationship, not price, and adapt the model carefully into Onitsha.`,

